In this lesson, we'll look at conditional probability, which is really just about updating what you believe after you learn something new. The big idea is the probability of A given B. Say you already know B happened, and you want the chance that A also happened. So your world shrinks to just B, and within that smaller world you ask how often A shows up too. Picture the sample space of all outcomes, but then you only look at the ones inside B, and you count the share of those that also land in A. There's also a handy rearrangement of the formula that lets you break a joint probability into a chain, where one event happens first and then the other follows conditionally. It's the same idea, just written in a different order. The intuition is simple: once you know B is true, B becomes your new playground, and A's chance is measured only inside it. Now let's see it in action.